WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.401 --> 00:00:03.103
And now, we briefly
interrupt our critique

00:00:03.169 --> 00:00:06.840
of the extra silly to salute
someone extra special.

00:00:06.906 --> 00:00:10.477
Now, if I suggested a sport
that literally drained your body

00:00:10.543 --> 00:00:12.479
of life sustaining
oxygen, edging

00:00:12.545 --> 00:00:14.848
you to the very
brink of existence,

00:00:14.914 --> 00:00:18.118
you'd probably say, no thanks.

00:00:18.184 --> 00:00:20.253
But then you're not
Alessia Zecchini,

00:00:20.320 --> 00:00:22.222
aiming to dive
deeper on one breath

00:00:22.288 --> 00:00:25.225
than any female freediver
has before in her category.

00:00:25.825 --> 00:00:26.526
[plop]

00:00:28.995 --> 00:00:31.998
Even at the surface, the average
person runs out of breath

00:00:32.065 --> 00:00:35.502
after little more than
a minute, but Alessia

00:00:35.568 --> 00:00:36.536
is far from average.

00:00:37.437 --> 00:00:39.239
[music playing]

00:00:42.675 --> 00:00:46.913
And down at 351 feet, almost
the length of a football

00:00:46.980 --> 00:00:50.150
field, she breaks the record,

00:00:53.553 --> 00:00:57.290
surfacing after a single breath
hold of 3 and 1/2 minutes.

00:00:57.357 --> 00:00:58.892
[cheering]

00:01:00.360 --> 00:01:03.096
It's easy to forget how
dangerous swimming under water

00:01:03.163 --> 00:01:04.597
can be.

00:01:04.664 --> 00:01:09.135
It can feel tranquil,
almost womb like,

00:01:11.304 --> 00:01:12.338
[thud]

00:01:12.405 --> 00:01:15.074
until you hit a problem,
which in free diving

00:01:15.141 --> 00:01:17.744
can result in oxygen
starvation, nitrogen

00:01:17.811 --> 00:01:20.013
narcosis,
decompression sickness,

00:01:20.079 --> 00:01:22.315
blackout, all the bad stuff.

00:01:22.382 --> 00:01:24.617
But while I wouldn't advise
trying to beat Alessia's

00:01:24.684 --> 00:01:26.619
record, I would
recommend learning

00:01:26.686 --> 00:01:28.321
the science behind the feat.

00:01:29.789 --> 00:01:33.393
As our freediver dives, his
body consumes its oxygen supply,

00:01:34.694 --> 00:01:36.229
which can lead to a
potentially lethal blackout.

00:01:38.031 --> 00:01:40.433
In his favor is an
evolutionary response

00:01:40.500 --> 00:01:43.470
called "the mammalian
dive reflex," which

00:01:43.536 --> 00:01:47.740
diverts oxygen-rich blood to
the vital organs and reduces

00:01:47.807 --> 00:01:50.677
the heart rate, lowering
oxygen consumption.

00:01:51.845 --> 00:01:54.514
Efficient monofin
technique also helps.

00:01:54.581 --> 00:01:57.984
Its large surface area
displaces a lot of water,

00:01:58.051 --> 00:02:00.320
propelling him forwards
with minimal effort,

00:02:00.386 --> 00:02:02.522
thereby conserving oxygen.

00:02:03.823 --> 00:02:06.526
OK, nearly ready to
break some records?

00:02:06.593 --> 00:02:08.461
It's time for a quick kit check.

00:02:10.163 --> 00:02:11.731
Flippers work on
the same principle

00:02:11.798 --> 00:02:15.835
as monofins, displacing
water to generate propulsion.

00:02:15.902 --> 00:02:16.736
[splash]

00:02:17.637 --> 00:02:20.106
Not so good on land, though.

00:02:20.173 --> 00:02:23.810
All right, let's dive down where
the mammalian dive reflex kicks

00:02:23.877 --> 00:02:27.313
in, even at shallow depths.

00:02:27.380 --> 00:02:29.215
[music playing]

00:02:30.517 --> 00:02:32.318
Not exactly what I meant
by mammalian dive reflex.

00:02:33.720 --> 00:02:36.689
And while they'll still be
experiencing the reflex,

00:02:36.756 --> 00:02:39.259
they're rather wasting its
oxygen-conserving effects.

00:02:41.094 --> 00:02:41.961
That's better.

00:02:42.028 --> 00:02:43.396
Making the most
of the propulsion

00:02:43.463 --> 00:02:46.666
generated by his flippers
with gentle rhythmic kicks,

00:02:46.733 --> 00:02:50.336
conserving precious oxygen
for the swim back up,

00:02:50.403 --> 00:02:52.739
or for riding a bike.

00:02:52.805 --> 00:02:57.176
OK, let's head up, but calmly
and oxygen efficiently so as

00:02:57.243 --> 00:02:59.913
to avoid that thing
all free divers fear.

00:03:01.247 --> 00:03:03.116
I was actually thinking
of "blacking out,"

00:03:03.182 --> 00:03:05.218
but on that evidence,
I think we should leave

00:03:05.285 --> 00:03:07.954
Alessia's record well alone.

